# rfc7606-relay-one-field -- a relayed UPDATE carries one NLRI-bearing field
#
# VALIDATES: AC-1 -- a mixed-shape UPDATE received from a route-server client is split before
# it is relayed, so each UPDATE the receiver sees carries at most one of the four fields named
# by RFC 7606 Section 5.1's second bullet: "An UPDATE message MUST NOT contain more than one
# of the following: non-empty Withdrawn Routes field, non-empty Network Layer Reachability
# Information field, MP_REACH_NLRI attribute, and MP_UNREACH_NLRI attribute."
#
# VALIDATES: AC-3 -- the mixed shape is still ACCEPTED on receive. Section 5.1 also requires
# that an implementation "MUST still be prepared to receive these fields in any position or
# combination", so the source peer must not be reset. A NOTIFICATION or a dropped session
# would fail this test at the exit code.
#
# AC-6 (withdrawals before announcements) is NOT asserted here, and the claim is left out
# rather than implied: Checker.check matches an arriving message against every pending rule,
# not just the head (internal/test/peer/checker.go:400-411), so declaration order in a .ci is
# not arrival order. Ordering is pinned by the unit tests instead
# (TestForwardSplitMixedShapeWithdrawalsFirst, TestSplitCompliantWithdrawalsPrecedeAnnouncements).
#
# PREVENTS: the relay reproducing a received mixed shape. The unit tests pin buildFwdBody and
# both splitters directly; this test is the one that proves the behavior survives the whole
# daemon path, from wire receive through the route server to the wire again.
#
# Two eBGP peers, following bgp-rs-reactor-fastpath.ci: source (65001) sends ONE UPDATE that
# both withdraws 172.16.0.0/24 and announces 10.0.0.0/24; receiver (65002) must see those as
# two separate UPDATEs.
#
# Deliberately WITHOUT `rs-fast-path enable`, unlike bgp-rs-reactor-fastpath.ci. Both settings
# reach the code under test (buildFwdBody is on both routes), but with the fast path enabled
# the receiver intermittently also gets a SECOND announce of 10.0.0.0/24 whose AS_PATH is
# [65001] -- no 65000 prepend and no ATTR_253 -- which matches no rule and fails the run.
# Measured: 12/16 passes with it, 20/20 without, and 4/4 mutant failures either way. The
# extra frame is not caused by this change (bgp-rs-reactor-fastpath.ci itself failed 1 in 6 on
# an unmodified tree, same EOR-versus-forward signature), so it is left to its own
# investigation rather than pinned here. What produces it is NOT established: the wire bytes
# are evidence, the producing function has not been read. An unprepended AS_PATH toward an
# eBGP peer would be a defect in its own right and is worth that investigation.

stdin=peer:terminator=EOF_PEER
option=tcp_connections:value=2
option=timeout:value=15s
option=bind:value=0.0.0.0
# Map conn=N to a peer by remote IP instead of accept order (peer_connmap.go), and handshake
# both peers before either script runs, so the forward below is not a race. Same reasoning as
# bgp-rs-reactor-fastpath.ci.
option=conn_map:value=remote-ip
# Wait for ze's End-of-RIB on BOTH connections before either script sends.
#
# The handshake alone is NOT the barrier this file's comment above assumed. It
# proves ze left OpenSent; it does not prove bgp-rs has registered the peer as a
# forward target. bgp-rs registers at the state=up event and captures its peer-up
# cut there, so a source UPDATE arriving before conn=2 is registered lands at or
# below that peer's cut and belongs to the Adj-RIB-In REPLAY -- which carries
# announcements only. The receiver then sees the announce and never the
# withdrawal this test asserts, which is exactly the intermittent failure above.
# ze emits the EoR only after that peer's initial sync, i.e. strictly after
# registration, so it is the first frame that proves the peer is a live target.
#
# Opt-in because consuming the EoR is only safe here: six of the nine conn_map
# tests declare it as an expectation and Checker.consumeMatches would be robbed
# of a frame they assert. This file declares none.
option=await_eor:value=true

# Source peer (conn=1): ONE UPDATE mixing Withdrawn Routes with NLRI.
#   withdrawn: 0004 18AC1000            -- 172.16.0.0/24
#   attrs:     001B ORIGIN=IGP, AS_PATH=[65001], NEXT_HOP=1.1.1.1, LOCAL_PREF=100
#   nlri:      180A0000                 -- 10.0.0.0/24
# Two NLRI-bearing fields in one message: exactly what Section 5.1 forbids a sender to emit,
# and exactly what an older speaker is allowed to send us.
action=send:conn=1:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF003A02000418AC1000001B4001010040020602010000FDE94003040101010140050400000064180A0000

# // test-relax: NEITHER peer declares an End-of-RIB expectation, and that is deliberate
# rather than an omission. Checker.check (internal/test/peer/checker.go:414-418) silently
# accepts an unmatched KEEPALIVE or EOR, precisely because initial route sync races the read
# loop. Declaring the EOR opts INTO that race instead of out of it: whichever of the EOR and
# the forwarded UPDATE arrives first meets the rule, and this test failed twice that way on an
# unchanged tree (once at the source, once at the receiver) before the rules were left
# undeclared. Not declaring them costs no coverage here: neither EOR is part of the RFC 7606
# shape behavior under test, and conn=1's action=send is what keeps that peer listening.

# Receiver peer (conn=2), seq=1: the withdrawal, ALONE, asserted byte for byte.
#   0004 18AC1000 0000  -- withdrawn 172.16.0.0/24, no attributes, no NLRI
# This is the load-bearing assertion. A withdraw-only UPDATE on this wire can only exist if
# the relay split the received message: before this change the withdrawal was relayed inside
# the same UPDATE as the announcement, which cannot match these bytes. Full-message equality
# is used rather than contains= so that a message carrying the withdrawal PLUS anything else
# fails.
expect=bgp:conn=2:seq=1:hex=FFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF001B02000418AC10000000

# Receiver peer (conn=2), seq=2: the announcement, in its own UPDATE.
# The asserted bytes are the forwarded route's AS_PATH and NEXT_HOP:
#   AS_PATH:  40020A02020000FDE80000FDE9 (AS_SEQUENCE[65000, 65001] -- ze prepended its AS)
#   NEXT_HOP: 40030401010101 (1.1.1.1)
# contains= rather than hex= for the same reason bgp-rs-reactor-fastpath.ci gives: the eBGP
# LOCAL_PREF rewrite is an open question settled elsewhere. These bytes cannot be satisfied by
# a withdraw, which carries no path attributes at all.
expect=bgp:conn=2:seq=2:contains=40020A02020000FDE80000FDE940030401010101
EOF_PEER

# Observer: shut down cleanly after forwarding completes.
tmpfs=shutdown-after-up.run:mode=755:terminator=EOF_RUN
#!/usr/bin/env python3
# // test-relax: the observer's synchronous `show bgp summary` eor-sent poll +
# `request shutdown` is replaced by ze_api.run_rs_observer -- event-driven readiness
# (subscribe + wait_for_event on the async channel) plus a fire-and-forget shutdown.
# A more robust rewrite, not a relaxation: same "wait until the RS replayed to every
# peer" semantics, no expect/action/reject assertion removed. The old poll's dispatch
# RPC could stall ~30s under load and strand the shutdown until ze was killed.
from ze_api import API

API().run_rs_observer(expected_peers=2, forward_prefix="10.0.0.0/24")
EOF_RUN

stdin=ze-bgp:terminator=EOF_CONF
plugin {
	internal rs {
		use bgp-rs
	}
	external shutdown-after-up {
		run ./shutdown-after-up.run
		encoder json
	}
}
bgp {
	peer source-peer {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.1
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.1
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65001
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}
	}
	peer receiver-peer {
		connection {
			remote {
				ip 127.0.0.2
			}
			local {
				ip 127.0.0.2
				accept false
			}
		}
		session {
			asn {
				local 65000
				remote 65002
			}
			router-id 1.2.3.4
			family {
				ipv4/unicast { prefix { maximum 10000; } }
			}
			capability {
				graceful-restart disable
			}
		}
		behavior {
			group-updates disable
		}
	}
}
EOF_CONF

cmd=background:seq=1:exec=ze-peer --port $PORT:stdin=peer
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze -:stdin=ze-bgp:timeout=15s
expect=exit:code=0
reject=stderr:pattern=panic
reject=stderr:pattern=fatal error
# The source must not be reset for sending a mixed shape: Section 5.1 requires receivers to
# accept these fields in any combination.
reject=stderr:pattern=notification
